    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Frederick took Silesia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the 1740s and kept it through the Seven Years' War against a coalition of Austria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, at a heavy cost in lives and money.</li><li>He called the sovereign the first servant of the state, reformed the courts and civil service, curbed judicial torture and invited settlers of any faith, while keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> favoured and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Catholics under limits.</li><li>In 1772 he annexed most of Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> and set out to Germanise its people, a side of his rule that sits beside his reputation as a patron of music, letters and science.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Frederick composed 121 sonatas for flute and continuo, and visitors to his grave at Sanssouci often leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> on the stone in honour of his promotion of the crop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

